WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.640 --> 00:00:04.690
First, let's build up
the conceptual mechanics

00:00:04.690 --> 00:00:07.420
for these new types
of random algorithms

00:00:07.420 --> 00:00:12.430
which accept some input
n, and if n is prime,

00:00:12.430 --> 00:00:16.390
our algorithm will output
prime with 100% certainty,

00:00:16.390 --> 00:00:21.030
and it will never
label it as composite.

00:00:21.030 --> 00:00:23.010
However, if n is
composite, then there

00:00:23.010 --> 00:00:25.250
will be some tiny
chance of error,

00:00:25.250 --> 00:00:29.870
e, that it will label it
prime, otherwise there is a 1

00:00:29.870 --> 00:00:32.240
minus this tiny
error probability

00:00:32.240 --> 00:00:36.580
that it will correctly
identify it as composite.

00:00:36.580 --> 00:00:38.130
So we'll start simple.

00:00:38.130 --> 00:00:41.360
Out of some universe of
integers up to some limit,

00:00:41.360 --> 00:00:45.720
we grab a number and
call this integer n.

00:00:45.720 --> 00:00:48.030
And we input n into our machine.

00:00:48.030 --> 00:00:51.060
And previously in our
trial division methods,

00:00:51.060 --> 00:00:54.540
we basically iterated
through all values

00:00:54.540 --> 00:00:57.740
from 1 to the square
root of n, and tested

00:00:57.740 --> 00:01:00.790
if that number divides in.

00:01:00.790 --> 00:01:05.069
And ideally, we only wanted
to check primes to save time.

00:01:05.069 --> 00:01:07.940
And if yes, a divides
n, then we know

00:01:07.940 --> 00:01:10.040
that n is a composite
number, because we

00:01:10.040 --> 00:01:12.000
found a composite witness.

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:14.810
If not, then we are not sure.

00:01:14.810 --> 00:01:19.660
So we go back and we increment
a, and we test again.

00:01:19.660 --> 00:01:22.780
And once we exhausted
all possible tests,

00:01:22.780 --> 00:01:28.010
we could then say yes, n is
prime if we found no divisors.

00:01:28.010 --> 00:01:31.220
But now let's be lazy.

00:01:31.220 --> 00:01:34.510
What if we just pick
a few random integers

00:01:34.510 --> 00:01:37.270
and do a few
divisibility tests, which

00:01:37.270 --> 00:01:40.030
you can think of as
random questions.

00:01:40.030 --> 00:01:44.240
Since we know that some
number n, if it's composite,

00:01:44.240 --> 00:01:47.930
it must have some
divisors scattered around.

00:01:47.930 --> 00:01:51.010
And at minimum, it
has a single divisor.

00:01:51.010 --> 00:01:53.230
And some composite numbers
have many divisors.

00:01:53.230 --> 00:01:57.120
So anyway, we picked a
random integer a between 1

00:01:57.120 --> 00:01:59.810
and the square root
of n, and that's it.

00:01:59.810 --> 00:02:03.820
Then we just checked
if a divides n.

00:02:03.820 --> 00:02:06.450
And as before, if a
divides n, then we

00:02:06.450 --> 00:02:08.350
know for sure that
n is composite.

00:02:08.350 --> 00:02:09.949
We found a witness.

00:02:09.949 --> 00:02:12.680
If not, then we haven't
learned too much,

00:02:12.680 --> 00:02:15.170
except that it could be prime.

00:02:15.170 --> 00:02:19.900
So to be safe, we could
generate a few more random

00:02:19.900 --> 00:02:22.450
a's, and keep testing.

00:02:22.450 --> 00:02:26.740
And perhaps after 100 or 1,000
iterations, we could stop

00:02:26.740 --> 00:02:30.390
and say it's probably
prime with some certainty.

00:02:30.390 --> 00:02:33.610
Say, for example, 99.9%.

00:02:33.610 --> 00:02:35.560
And this is similar
to the example

00:02:35.560 --> 00:02:38.160
game on conditional probability.

00:02:38.160 --> 00:02:40.010
In the simplest
version, we were trying

00:02:40.010 --> 00:02:45.050
to guess if a coin was fair,
or if it was a two headed coin.

00:02:45.050 --> 00:02:48.610
In this case, tails is
like finding a divisor.

00:02:48.610 --> 00:02:50.990
It's a witness of a fair coin.

00:02:50.990 --> 00:02:53.510
Heads is the case where
we might want the person

00:02:53.510 --> 00:02:55.560
to flip again and iterate.

00:02:55.560 --> 00:02:59.360
In this case, after around five
heads, we are more than 90%

00:02:59.360 --> 00:03:01.880
sure, so we could
stop and say, oh, we

00:03:01.880 --> 00:03:03.640
think the coin is two headed.

00:03:06.620 --> 00:03:09.990
So here's a program I've set
up which compares our old trial

00:03:09.990 --> 00:03:14.780
division methods with this
new random division test.

00:03:14.780 --> 00:03:17.610
And I'm specifically using the
current trial division speed

00:03:17.610 --> 00:03:20.260
leader, which is
a program by Dino,

00:03:20.260 --> 00:03:22.710
and I posted the link in
the header of the program.

00:03:25.380 --> 00:03:28.290
So to begin, notice the
variable number of trials.

00:03:28.290 --> 00:03:30.200
This is the number
of random guesses.

00:03:30.200 --> 00:03:33.830
So we'll start at something
small, such as three.

00:03:33.830 --> 00:03:38.870
And notice even with small
input, if the input is prime,

00:03:38.870 --> 00:03:43.150
the random division algorithm
will always output prime.

00:03:43.150 --> 00:03:45.700
But when the input
is composite, we

00:03:45.700 --> 00:03:49.260
see the random division can
make mistakes and identify it

00:03:49.260 --> 00:03:51.750
incorrectly as prime.

00:03:51.750 --> 00:03:54.090
However, we can fix
this by increasing

00:03:54.090 --> 00:03:57.060
the number of trials, and then
the probability of an error

00:03:57.060 --> 00:03:58.090
goes down.

00:03:58.090 --> 00:04:01.175
And we see now that the
outputs more or less match.

00:04:06.940 --> 00:04:09.480
And as I test larger
input, the error

00:04:09.480 --> 00:04:11.820
grows again, so I
need to increase

00:04:11.820 --> 00:04:15.480
the number of random
tests accordingly.

00:04:15.480 --> 00:04:18.810
And when I do, the
outputs match very nicely.

00:04:18.810 --> 00:04:19.884
They seem identical.

00:04:24.240 --> 00:04:27.190
But with huge input
size, I need thousands

00:04:27.190 --> 00:04:30.050
of random tests for
this to be accurate.

00:04:30.050 --> 00:04:32.890
So we haven't actually improved
the number of steps needed.

00:04:32.890 --> 00:04:36.840
Our trial division method
still seems better.

00:04:36.840 --> 00:04:40.220
This is because the error
rate of the division test

00:04:40.220 --> 00:04:43.250
is so high, but we are close.

00:04:43.250 --> 00:04:45.540
We have the right idea.

00:04:45.540 --> 00:04:48.650
So we need to use another test.

00:04:48.650 --> 00:04:51.170
We need an equation, which
is fast to calculate,

00:04:51.170 --> 00:04:54.970
that can be used to prove
whether a number is composite.

00:04:54.970 --> 00:04:59.330
And it must accept not only
the input integer n, but also

00:04:59.330 --> 00:05:02.650
a random integer a
and do a random test

00:05:02.650 --> 00:05:04.539
in the same sort of way.

